**Key idea:** keep last cycle's value in one flop and compare it with the current value. The difference lasts exactly one clock, which gives a one-cycle pulse.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk",  "wave": "p........" },
  { "name": "d",    "wave": "0.1...0.." },
  { "name": "d_q",  "wave": "0..1...0." },
  { "name": "rise", "wave": "0.10....." },
  { "name": "fall", "wave": "0.....10." },
  { "name": "any",  "wave": "0.10..10." }
],
  "head": { "text": "rise = d & ~d_q, fall = ~d & d_q, any = d ^ d_q" } }
```

### Code
```systemverilog
module edge_det #(parameter bit ASYNC_IN = 1) (
  input  logic clk, rst_n, d,
  output logic rise, fall, any
);
  logic s1, s2, d_s, d_q;
  // optional 2-FF synchronizer when d comes from another clock domain
  always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n)
    if (!rst_n) {s2, s1} <= '0;
    else        {s2, s1} <= {s1, d};
  assign d_s = ASYNC_IN ? s2 : d;

  always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n)
    if (!rst_n) d_q <= 1'b0;
    else        d_q <= d_s;

  assign rise = d_s & ~d_q;     // pulse when req rises
  assign fall = ~d_s & d_q;
  assign any  = d_s ^ d_q;
endmodule
```
Synthesized circuit: one DFF plus an AND gate with an inverted input (or an XOR for `any`).

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- **Asynchronous input:** detect on the synchronized copy, never on the raw pin. A metastable `d` could make `rise` and the logic that uses it disagree.
- Reset value of `d_q`: if `d` is already 1 coming out of reset, a reset value of 0 gives one false `rise`. Decide which behavior the spec wants.
- The outputs are combinational. Register them if they drive another block or leave the module.

### Follow-ups
- *Registered pulse?* `rise_q <= d_s & ~d_q;` gives the same one-cycle pulse, one cycle later and glitch-free.
- *SVA check:* `assert property (@(posedge clk) rise |=> !rise);` and `$rose(d_s) |-> rise`.

**Key idea:** an odd divider can't get 50% duty from rising edges alone, because the period is 3 clock cycles and the high time must be 1.5. Use a mod-3 counter, make one signal on the **posedge**, a second on the **negedge** (half a period later), and combine them.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk",      "wave": "lhlhlhlhlhlhlhlhlh" },
  { "name": "cnt",      "wave": "==.=.=.=.=.=.=.=.=", "data": ["0", "1", "2", "0", "1", "2", "0", "1", "2", "0"] },
  { "name": "pos (posedge, toggles at cnt==0)", "wave": "01.....0.....1...." },
  { "name": "neg (negedge, toggles at cnt==2)", "wave": "0...1.....0.....1." },
  { "name": "clk_div3 = pos ^ neg", "wave": "01..0..1..0..1..0." }
],
  "head": { "text": "each character is half a clk period: output is high 1.5 cycles out of 3" } }
```

### Code
```systemverilog
module div3_50 (input logic clk, rst_n, output logic clk_div3);
  logic [1:0] cnt;
  logic       pos, neg;

  always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n)           // mod-3 counter
    if (!rst_n)        cnt <= '0;
    else if (cnt == 2) cnt <= '0;
    else               cnt <= cnt + 1'b1;

  always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n)           // toggles every 3 rising edges
    if (!rst_n)        pos <= 1'b0;
    else if (cnt == 0) pos <= ~pos;

  always_ff @(negedge clk or negedge rst_n)           // NEGEDGE: half a period later
    if (!rst_n)        neg <= 1'b0;
    else if (cnt == 2) neg <= ~neg;

  assign clk_div3 = pos ^ neg;                         // 1.5T high, 1.5T low
endmodule
```

Equivalent form: `pos <= (cnt == 2)` gives one cycle high out of 3. Then `neg <= pos` on the negedge, and `clk_div3 = pos | neg` gives 1 + 0.5 = 1.5 cycles high.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- If both flops use the posedge, the XOR only changes on rising edges, so the duty is 2/3 or 1/3, not 50%. That is the most common mistake (and the bug in the first draft of this answer, which also dropped a `;` after `cnt`).
- The output comes from combinational logic (XOR/OR). Make sure the two inputs never change at the same instant. Here they change half a period apart, so the output can't glitch.
- 50% duty from the negedge depends on the input clock duty being 50%.

### Follow-ups
- *Even N:* toggle a flop every N/2 cycles. Divide-by-2 is just `q <= ~q`.
- *STA:* declare the output with `create_generated_clock -divide_by 3` (or `-edges`), or timing on that clock is wrong.
- *Clock gating:* use an ICG cell (latch + AND), not a plain AND gate, so an enable change can't chop the clock.

**Key idea:** `$cast(dst, src)` is a **run-time checked assignment**. You need it when the compiler can't prove the assignment is legal: a downcast from a base handle to a derived handle, or an integer to an enum.

```systemverilog
Base  b = Child::new();     // handle type Base, object type Child
Child c;
// c = b;                   // compile error: a downcast needs a check
if (!$cast(c, b))           // function form: returns 1 or 0
  `uvm_error("CAST", "b is not a Child")

// everyday UVM use: clone() returns uvm_object
my_pkt p2;
if (!$cast(p2, p1.clone())) `uvm_fatal("CAST", "clone type mismatch")

// int -> enum, with a range check
typedef enum {IDLE, BUSY, DONE} st_e;
st_e s;
if (!$cast(s, 5)) $display("5 is not a legal st_e");
```

| Form | On success | On failure |
| --- | --- | --- |
| function: `if (!$cast(d, s))` | returns 1, assigns | returns 0, `d` unchanged, no error printed |
| task: `$cast(d, s);` | assigns | **run-time error** |
| static cast: `st_e'(5)` | — | no check at all (illegal value stored) |

### Explanation
- **Upcast** (derived → base) never needs `$cast`; it is always legal.
- **Downcast** succeeds only if the *object* really is that type (or a subclass of it). The handle type doesn't matter.
- A null source handle casts successfully to null.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Ignoring the function's return value leaves `dst` null or stale, and the crash happens later with a confusing message.
- A static cast `type'(x)` is compile-time and unchecked. Use `$cast` when the value might be out of range.

### Follow-ups
- *Where else in UVM?* Analysis imps that receive a base type, a driver that handles several item types (`if ($cast(r, req)) drive_read(r);`), and `uvm_reg_adapter::bus2reg()`.

**Key idea:** an **abstract class** defines an interface that subclasses must fill in, and can't be created itself. A **singleton** guarantees exactly one instance, shared through a static accessor.

```systemverilog
// abstract class: common API, no direct instances
virtual class base_scoreboard;
  pure virtual function bit compare(uvm_object exp, uvm_object act);
  function void report(); /* shared code */ endfunction
endclass
class pkt_scoreboard extends base_scoreboard;
  virtual function bit compare(uvm_object exp, uvm_object act);
    return exp.compare(act);
  endfunction
endclass
// base_scoreboard s = new();   // compile error: abstract

// singleton: one global instance
class cfg_mgr;
  local static cfg_mgr inst;
  local function new(); endfunction        // nobody outside can call new()
  static function cfg_mgr get();
    if (inst == null) inst = new();          // lazily created on first use
    return inst;
  endfunction
endclass
// cfg_mgr m = cfg_mgr::get();              // same object everywhere
```

|   | Abstract (`virtual class`) | Singleton |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Purpose | force a common API on all subclasses | one shared instance |
| Instantiable | no (only subclasses) | once, via `get()` |
| Key syntax | `pure virtual` methods | `local` constructor + `static get()` |
| UVM examples | `uvm_object`, `uvm_component` conceptually; user base classes | `uvm_root`, factory, report server, `uvm_coreservice_t` |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- A non-abstract subclass that doesn't implement every `pure virtual` method is a compile error.
- A singleton is global state, so tests that change it affect each other within one simulation.

### Follow-ups
- *Interface class?* `interface class` with `implements` allows multiple inheritance of APIs (SV-2012).
- *Why `local` on `new()`?* Otherwise anyone could create a second instance.

**Key idea:** a **clocking block** gives the testbench race-free timing at the clock edge: sample just *before* the edge, drive just *after* it. A **virtual interface** is the bridge from dynamic class objects (driver, monitor) to the static interface instance that connects to the DUT.

```wavedrom
