### Code
```systemverilog
interface ahb_if (input logic hclk);
  logic [31:0] haddr, hwdata, hrdata;
  logic        hready;
  clocking drv_cb @(posedge hclk);
    default input #1step output #1;      // sample before the edge, drive 1 time unit after
    output haddr, hwdata;
    input  hready, hrdata;
  endclocking
  clocking mon_cb @(posedge hclk);
    default input #1step;
    input haddr, hwdata, hready, hrdata;
  endclocking
  modport DRV (clocking drv_cb);
  modport MON (clocking mon_cb);
endinterface

class ahb_driver extends uvm_driver #(ahb_item);
  virtual ahb_if vif;                     // handle to the static interface
  task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    forever begin
      seq_item_port.get_next_item(req);
      @(vif.drv_cb);
      vif.drv_cb.haddr <= req.addr;       // synchronous drive through the clocking block
      do @(vif.drv_cb); while (!vif.drv_cb.hready);
      seq_item_port.item_done();
    end
  endtask
endclass
// tb_top: uvm_config_db#(virtual ahb_if)::set(null, "*", "vif", ahb_if_inst);
```

### Explanation
- **Without a clocking block,** a TB that drives at `@(posedge clk)` with blocking assignments may change the input in the same time step the DUT samples it. The result depends on scheduling order.
- **modport** restricts directions per user (driver vs monitor), so wrong-direction drives fail at compile time.
- **Why "virtual":** an interface is elaborated hardware (static); objects are created at run time. A class can't *contain* an interface, only point to one.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Driving clocking-block outputs requires `<=` (synchronous drive). Reading a signal directly (`vif.hready`) instead of `vif.drv_cb.hready` bypasses the sampling and brings races back.
- A null `vif` (config_db `get()` failed) crashes later. Check `get()` and `uvm_fatal`.

### Follow-ups
- *What is `#1step`?* One time-precision step before the edge, i.e. the Preponed-region value.
- *Can monitors use a clocking block too?* Yes, input-only (`mon_cb`), so they see exactly what the DUT saw.

**Key idea:** read UVM code for the five classic failure classes: **factory registration, config_db, phase kinds, the driver/sequence handshake, and object creation/randomization**.

```systemverilog
class my_driver extends uvm_driver #(my_item);
  my_if vif;                                              // (1)
  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent);
  endfunction                                             // (2) no `uvm_component_utils
  function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);             // (8) no super.build_phase
    uvm_config_db#(virtual my_if)::get(this, "", "vif", vif);   // (3) unchecked
  endfunction
  function void run_phase(uvm_phase phase);               // (4) function, but it waits
    forever begin
      seq_item_port.get_next_item(req);
      @(posedge vif.clk);
      vif.data <= req.data;
    end                                                   // (5) no item_done()
  endfunction
endclass

class my_seq extends uvm_sequence #(my_item);
  `uvm_object_utils(my_seq)                               // (6) no new(name)
  task body();
    my_item it = new("it");                               // (7) new() + no randomize
    start_item(it);
    finish_item(it);
  endtask
endclass
```

| # | Bug | Consequence | Fix |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | `my_if vif;` | compile error: a class can't contain an interface | `virtual my_if vif;` |
| 2 | no `` `uvm_component_utils `` | factory can't create or override it | add the macro |
| 3 | `get()` not checked | null vif, crash later with a confusing message | `if (!get(...)) `uvm_fatal(...)` |
| 4 | `run_phase` is a function | compile error: a function can't wait on `@` | `task run_phase` |
| 5 | no `item_done()` | `finish_item()` never returns: hang | `seq_item_port.item_done();` |
| 6 | no constructor in `my_seq` | macro calls `new(name)`: compile error | add `new(string name = "my_seq")` |
| 7 | `new()`, no randomize | overrides ignored, item never randomized | `type_id::create` + `randomize()` |
| 8 | no `super.build_phase` | auto-config of fields skipped | call it first |

### Fixed code
```systemverilog
class my_driver extends uvm_driver #(my_item);
  `uvm_component_utils(my_driver)
  virtual my_if vif;
  function new(string name, uvm_component parent); super.new(name, parent); endfunction
  function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    super.build_phase(phase);
    if (!uvm_config_db#(virtual my_if)::get(this, "", "vif", vif))
      `uvm_fatal("NOVIF", "virtual interface not set")
  endfunction
  task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    forever begin
      seq_item_port.get_next_item(req);
      @(posedge vif.clk);
      vif.data <= req.data;
      seq_item_port.item_done();
    end
  endtask
endclass

class my_seq extends uvm_sequence #(my_item);
  `uvm_object_utils(my_seq)
  function new(string name = "my_seq"); super.new(name); endfunction
  task body();
    my_item it = my_item::type_id::create("it");
    start_item(it);
    if (!it.randomize()) `uvm_error("RND", "randomize failed")
    finish_item(it);
  endtask
endclass
```

### Follow-ups
- *Better driving style?* Use a clocking block (`@(vif.cb); vif.cb.data <= …`) to avoid races.
- *Where does `req` come from?* `uvm_driver #(REQ)` declares `req` and `rsp` members.

**Key idea:** five one-liners. Read declarations right to left, and know which operations are undefined behavior.

| Topic | Rule |
| --- | --- |
| `const int *p` | pointer to const int: can't change `*p`, can repoint `p` |
| `int *const p` | const pointer: can't repoint, can change `*p` |
| `const int *const p` | neither |
| pass by value | callee gets a copy; changes are invisible to the caller |
| pass by pointer / reference | callee can modify the caller's object; `const T&` avoids the copy without allowing changes |
| virtual destructor | needed in any base class deleted through a base pointer |
| `memcpy` vs `memmove` | `memcpy` on overlapping buffers is undefined; `memmove` handles overlap |
| `struct` vs `class` (C++) | only default access differs: `struct` public, `class` private |

### Code
```cpp
#include <cstring>
#include <cstdio>

void swap_ptr(int *a, int *b) { int t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t; }
void swap_ref(int &a, int &b) { int t = a;  a = b;  b = t; }

struct Base {
  virtual ~Base() { std::puts("~Base"); }        // without virtual: ~Derived is skipped
};
struct Derived : Base {
  int *buf = new int[100];
  ~Derived() override { delete[] buf; std::puts("~Derived"); }
};

int main() {
  int x = 1, y = 2;
  swap_ptr(&x, &y);                              // x=2, y=1
  swap_ref(x, y);                                // back to x=1, y=2

  const int *p = &x;  /* *p = 5;  error */  p = &y;   // OK: repoint
  int *const q = &x;  *q = 5;   /* q = &y; error */   // OK: modify

  Base *b = new Derived;
  delete b;                                      // prints ~Derived then ~Base

  char s[] = "abcdef";
  std::memmove(s + 2, s, 4);                     // overlapping: memmove is required
  std::printf("%s\n", s);                        // "ababcd"
}
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Without a virtual destructor, `delete base_ptr` is undefined behavior; in practice the derived destructor doesn't run and its resources leak.
- `memcpy`/`strcpy` without checking the destination size is the classic buffer overflow.
- A reference can't be null or re-seated; a pointer can.

### Follow-ups
- *Rule of three/five?* If a class needs a custom destructor, it probably needs a custom copy constructor and copy assignment too (plus move versions).
- *Link to SV:* SV class handles behave like C++ pointers to objects, and SV has no destructors (garbage collection).

**Key idea:** "unique" just means **every pair differs**. Write the pairwise constraint directly, or skip the solver: fill distinct values procedurally and **shuffle**.

```systemverilog
class uniq_arr;
  rand bit [7:0] arr[10];
  // Method 1: pairwise constraint, O(N^2) constraints
  constraint c_uniq {
    foreach (arr[i]) foreach (arr[j])
      if (i < j) arr[i] != arr[j];
  }
endclass

class uniq_perm;
  bit [7:0] arr[10];                     // not rand: filled in post_randomize
  // Method 2: a permutation of 0..N-1, O(N)
  function void post_randomize();
    foreach (arr[i]) arr[i] = i;
    arr.shuffle();
  endfunction
endclass

class uniq_any;                          // distinct random values from a large range
  rand bit [7:0] arr[10];
  function void post_randomize();
    bit seen[bit [7:0]];
    foreach (arr[i]) begin
      do arr[i] = $urandom; while (seen.exists(arr[i]));
      seen[arr[i]] = 1;
    end
  endfunction
endclass
```

| Method | Cost | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `unique {arr};` | solver | the easy answer when allowed |
| pairwise `!=` | N(N−1)/2 constraints (45 for N = 10) | stays inside the solver, works with other constraints |
| fill + `shuffle()` | O(N) | fast, but other constraints on `arr` can't interact |
| `randc` element | per variable | `randc` cycles one variable's values, not array elements against each other |

Example of method 2 for N = 5: `0 1 2 3 4` → `shuffle()` → `3 0 4 1 2`.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- The range must have at least N values: `bit [2:0]` (8 values) can't hold 10 unique elements, so randomize fails.
- The `if (i < j)` guard halves the constraints and avoids the impossible `arr[i] != arr[i]`.
- With the shuffle method, values decided in `post_randomize` are invisible to other constraints solved earlier.

### Follow-ups
- *randc behavior without randc?* Keep a shuffled list of all values and pop one per call, refilling and reshuffling when empty.
- *Unique within a sliding window only?* See the "5 consecutive values must differ" question.

**Key idea:** count occurrences with `sum() with (item == v)`, and forbid adjacent zeros pairwise. For speed, **randomize the gaps between zeros** instead of 300 elements.

### Direct constraint solution
```systemverilog
class arr300;
  rand bit [2:0] arr[300];
  int vals[6] = '{0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
  constraint c_val  { foreach (arr[i]) arr[i] inside {[0:5]}; }
  constraint c_cnt  { foreach (vals[v]) arr.sum() with (int'(item == vals[v])) == 50; }
  constraint c_no00 { foreach (arr[i]) if (i > 0) !(arr[i] == 0 && arr[i-1] == 0); }
endclass
```
Correct, but 6 counting constraints over 300 variables plus 299 adjacency constraints is a large problem for the solver.

### Faster: randomize the gaps
50 zeros split the 250 non-zero values into 51 gaps. The 49 inner gaps must hold at least 1 value each, so no two zeros touch.

```systemverilog
class arr300_fast;
  bit [2:0] arr[300];
  rand int unsigned gap[51];             // non-zeros before zero 0, between zeros, after zero 49
  constraint c_gap {
    foreach (gap[i]) gap[i] <= 250;
    foreach (gap[i]) if (i > 0 && i < 50) gap[i] >= 1;   // no two 0s adjacent
    gap.sum() with (int'(item)) == 250;
  }
  function void post_randomize();
    bit [2:0] nz[$];
    int k = 0;
    for (int v = 1; v <= 5; v++) repeat (50) nz.push_back(v);
    nz.shuffle();                        // 250 non-zero values in random order
    for (int i = 0; i <= 50; i++) begin
      repeat (gap[i]) arr[k++] = nz.pop_front();
      if (i < 50) arr[k++] = 0;
    end                                  // k == 300
  endfunction
endclass
```

Small example (6 elements, values 0–2, each twice, no 0–0): gaps = `[1, 1, 2]` and non-zeros shuffled to `2 1 1 2` give `2 0 1 0 1 2`.

| Approach | Solver variables | Speed | Distribution |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| direct constraints | 300 | slow | uniform over legal arrays |
| gaps + shuffle | 51 | fast | close to uniform, but not exactly |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- `int'(item == vals[v])`: without the cast the comparison is 1 bit and the sum overflows.
- Check feasibility: 50 zeros need at least 49 separators, and there are 250 non-zeros, so it's satisfiable.

### Follow-ups
- *Shuffle-and-repair:* shuffle the full multiset, then for each 0–0 pair swap the second 0 with a non-zero whose neighbors are both non-zero. That's simple, but needs a loop that terminates.

**Key idea:** apply a `unique` constraint to **every window of 5**. A window starting at `i` exists only while `i + 4` is still inside the array.

```systemverilog
class win5;
  rand bit [3:0] da[];
  constraint c_size { da.size() inside {[5:32]}; }
  constraint c_win {
    foreach (da[i]) if (i + 4 < da.size())
      unique {da[i], da[i+1], da[i+2], da[i+3], da[i+4]};
  }
  // without 'unique': every pair at distance 1..4 differs
  // foreach (da[i]) foreach (da[j]) if (i < j && j - i <= 4) da[i] != da[j];
endclass
```

Example result (size 9):

| index | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 | 8 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| value | 3 | 7 | 1 | 9 | 0 | 3 | 7 | 2 | 1 |

Windows `[0..4] = 3 7 1 9 0`, `[1..5] = 7 1 9 0 3`, … all unique. `3` reappears at index 5, which is fine because it's 5 apart.

### Explanation
- The pairwise version shows the real meaning: elements closer than 5 positions apart must differ.
- `if (i + 4 < da.size())` guards against indexing past the end. Without it, the solver reports an out-of-bounds error.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- The value range needs at least 5 distinct values, or randomize fails.
- With `da.size() < 5`, there are no windows and anything is legal. Decide whether the spec allows that.

### Follow-ups
- *Window of K as a parameter?* `foreach (da[i]) foreach (da[j]) if (i < j && j - i < K) da[i] != da[j];`.
- *No value repeated twice in a row?* That's the K = 2 case: `da[i] != da[i-1]`.

**Key idea:** most "puzzle" constraints are **relations between neighbors or pairs**. State variables (non-rand, including `static`) carry history from one `randomize()` call to the next.

```systemverilog
// 1. strictly increasing
rand bit [7:0] a[8];
constraint c_inc { foreach (a[i]) if (i > 0) a[i] > a[i-1]; }

// 2. alternate READ / WRITE across transactions
class bus_tr;
  rand bit   is_wr;
  static bit last_wr = 1;                         // shared history (state, not rand)
  constraint c_alt { is_wr != last_wr; }
  function void post_randomize(); last_wr = is_wr; endfunction
endclass

// 3. Sudoku: every row, column and 3x3 box holds 1..9 exactly once
class sudoku;
  rand bit [3:0] g[9][9];
  constraint c_sudoku {
    foreach (g[r, c]) g[r][c] inside {[1:9]};
    foreach (g[r]) unique {g[r]};                                   // rows
    foreach (g[r, c]) foreach (g[r2, c2])                           // columns and boxes
      if (r < r2 && (c == c2 || (r/3 == r2/3 && c/3 == c2/3))) g[r][c] != g[r2][c2];
  }
endclass
```

| Pattern | Constraint type | Trace / example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| strictly increasing | neighbor relation | `3 17 40 41 90 128 200 255` |
| alternating R/W | state variable + `post_randomize` | `last_wr` starts at 1, so successive calls give R, W, R, W, … |
| Sudoku | pairwise `!=` over rows/cols/boxes | any valid 9×9 grid |

### Explanation
- **Increasing:** with 8 elements in `[0:255]` it's easily satisfiable. With 300 elements of 8 bits it's impossible (only 256 values).
- **Alternation:** `static` makes the history shared by all `bus_tr` objects (one bus). Use a non-static member to alternate per object. `post_randomize` updates it only when randomize succeeds.
- **Sudoku:** the `r < r2` guard covers each pair once. Rows are handled by `unique`. Same-row pairs inside a box are already covered by the row constraint.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- A static state variable is shared across *all* instances, including other tests in the same simulation, so reset it when needed.
- Sudoku with 81 variables and many `!=` constraints is slow on some solvers. Pre-filling a few cells (from a puzzle) makes it a solver *for* Sudoku.

### Follow-ups
- *Non-decreasing?* Use `>=`.
- *Alternate in a sequence instead?* Keep the state in the sequence and use `randomize() with { is_wr != local::last; }`.

