questions.solution:
**Key idea:** an APB transfer is **SETUP** (PSEL=1, PENABLE=0) for exactly one cycle, then **ACCESS** (PSEL=1, PENABLE=1) until PREADY. Write one property per transition, plus stability during wait states.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "pclk",    "wave": "p......." },
  { "name": "psel",    "wave": "01...0.." },
  { "name": "penable", "wave": "0.1..0.." },
  { "name": "pready",  "wave": "0...10.." },
  { "name": "paddr",   "wave": "x=...x..", "data": ["A"] },
  { "name": "pwrite",  "wave": "x1...x.." },
  { "name": "phase",   "wave": "x===.x..", "data": ["SETUP", "ACCESS (wait)", "ACCESS (done)"] }
],
  "head": { "text": "1 setup cycle, access with one wait state, completion when psel && penable && pready" } }
```

### Code
```systemverilog
module apb_sva (input logic pclk, presetn, psel, penable, pready, pwrite,
                input logic [31:0] paddr, pwdata);
  default clocking @(posedge pclk); endclocking
  default disable iff (!presetn);

  // SETUP is followed by ACCESS
  a_setup_access: assert property (psel && !penable |=> psel && penable);
  // wait states: everything held
  a_wait_stable:  assert property (psel && penable && !pready |=>
                                   psel && penable && $stable({paddr, pwrite, pwdata}));
  // transfer ends: PENABLE drops
  a_end:          assert property (penable && pready |=> !penable);
  // PENABLE never without PSEL
  a_en_needs_sel: assert property (penable |-> psel);
  // no X on control while selected
  a_no_x:         assert property (psel |-> !$isunknown({paddr, pwrite, penable}));

  c_wait_state:   cover property (psel && penable && !pready);
  c_back2back:    cover property (penable && pready ##1 psel && !penable);
endmodule
bind apb_slave apb_sva u_apb_sva (.*);
```

### Explanation
- Completion is `psel && penable && pready`, not just `pready`, because PREADY may be held high by default.
- Back-to-back transfers go ACCESS → SETUP directly (PSEL stays high, PENABLE drops), which `a_end` allows.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- `$stable` across the setup → access edge isn't required by `a_wait_stable`; add `psel && !penable |=> $stable({paddr, pwrite, pwdata})` to cover that edge too.
- A slave that never asserts PREADY hangs the bus. Add a bounded liveness check (`psel && penable |-> ##[0:15] pready`) if the spec has a timeout.

### Follow-ups
- *PSLVERR?* Only valid in the last access cycle: `pslverr |-> psel && penable && pready`.

**Key idea:** properties can have **local variables**. Capture `$realtime` at one edge, then compare it at the next edge. The property is clocked by the signal being measured.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk (nominal 10 ns)", "wave": "lhlhlhllhl" },
  { "name": "period check",        "wave": "x=.=.==..=", "data": ["10 ns ok", "10 ns ok", "", "FAIL: 15 ns"] },
  {},
  { "name": "sig",                 "wave": "0.1..0.10." },
  { "name": "pulse width",         "wave": "x.=..x.=x.", "data": ["6 ns ok", "1 ns GLITCH"] }
],
  "head": { "text": "one cycle stretched to 15 ns; one high pulse only 1 ns wide (not to scale)" } }
```

### Code
```systemverilog
module clk_checks #(parameter realtime PERIOD = 10ns) (input logic clk, sig, rst_n);
  property p_period;
    realtime t;
    @(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
    (1, t = $realtime) |=>
      ($realtime - t >= 0.95 * PERIOD) && ($realtime - t <= 1.05 * PERIOD);
  endproperty
  a_period: assert property (p_period)
    else $error("clk period out of range");

  property p_min_high;                         // high pulse >= 2.5 ns
    realtime t;
    @(posedge sig) (1, t = $realtime) |-> @(negedge sig) ($realtime - t >= 2.5ns);
  endproperty
  property p_min_low;                          // low pulse >= 2.5 ns
    realtime t;
    @(negedge sig) (1, t = $realtime) |-> @(posedge sig) ($realtime - t >= 2.5ns);
  endproperty
  a_min_high: assert property (p_min_high) else $error("high glitch on sig");
  a_min_low:  assert property (p_min_low)  else $error("low glitch on sig");
endmodule
```

### Explanation
- `(1, t = $realtime)` is a sequence that always matches and assigns the local variable as a side effect.
- `|=>` on `@(posedge clk)` means "at the next rising edge", so `$realtime - t` is exactly one period.
- The glitch properties are **multi-clocked**: the antecedent runs on `posedge sig` and the consequent on the following `negedge sig`.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Use `realtime`/`$realtime` (not `time`/`$time`), or fractional ns are truncated by the timescale.
- If `PERIOD` comes from another domain or changes at run time (DVFS), sample it into a variable that stays stable during the check, or do the check in a monitor instead of SVA.
- Gated or stopped clocks fail the period check. Disable it (`disable iff (!clk_en)`) while gating is legal.

### Follow-ups
- *Duty cycle?* Capture at posedge, compare at negedge against `0.5 * PERIOD ± tolerance`.
- *Frequency ratio between two clocks?* Count edges of one clock per N edges of the other in a monitor.
